    "narrative": "The Zurich Zebras enter today\u2019s slate with a potent offensive alignment, headlined by **Yordan Alvarez** (74.93) and **Christian Yelich** (74.33). **Yordan Alvarez** remains the focal point of the lineup, boasting an elite 0.478 xwOBA, while **Christian Yelich** benefits significantly from a favorable matchup against Sugano (5.12 xERA) and a prime leadoff spot. We are also leaning heavily into the speed and efficiency of **Caleb Durbin** (67.91) and **Lars Nootbaar** (65.90); **Caleb Durbin** exploits a high-upside matchup against Yesavage, and while **Lars Nootbaar** remains a strong play, we are monitoring a 42% rain risk in his contest that could impact his total at-bats.\n\nThe most difficult decision on the bench involved **Carson Benge** (51.93), who was the final omission from our starting nine. While **Carson Benge** maintains solid metrics against Sasaki, his 51.93 efficiency score was narrowly eclipsed by the combination of platoon advantages and lineup positioning held by the likes of **Casey Schmitt** (63.20) and **Junior Caminero** (63.38). **Casey Schmitt** is currently elevated by wind-out conditions and a high-leverage matchup against Grayson Rodriguez (5.27 xERA), making him a superior play despite the lingering uncertainty of his batting order.\n\nWe are exercising caution with **Trea Turner** (52.92) and **Kyle Schwarber** (50.54) today, as both face the difficult task of solving Schlittler (3.02 xERA). Despite their individual talents, the elite SP skill of their opponent drags their efficiency scores toward our marginal threshold. Conversely, we continue to stash **Hyeseong Kim** while he remains in the minors, and we are closely watching the performance of **Dillon Dingler** (41.89), whose limited efficiency against Way (3.75 xERA) places him near our functional floor. Owners should note that **Pedro Ramirez** (35.82) was excluded today specifically for failing to meet our required 40.0 internal efficiency standard.",
